<Window x:Class="RPEReader.App.Views.HelpWindow"
        xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
        xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
        Title="RPE Reader — quick guide"
        Height="600" Width="680"
        WindowStartupLocation="CenterOwner"
        ShowInTaskbar="False">
  <Grid Margin="16">
    <Grid.RowDefinitions>
      <RowDefinition Height="*" />
      <RowDefinition Height="Auto" />
    </Grid.RowDefinitions>

    <ScrollViewer Grid.Row="0" VerticalScrollBarVisibility="Auto">
      <StackPanel>

        <TextBlock Text="Opening a file" Style="{StaticResource SectionHeader}" FontSize="15" />
        <TextBlock TextWrapping="Wrap" Margin="0,0,0,12">
          Use <Bold>File ▸ Open</Bold> (Ctrl+O), drag a single .rpe file onto the window, or — once the
          installer has registered the file association — double-click a .rpe file in Explorer.
          The file is opened read-only; see <Italic>Editing a file</Italic> below to change it.
        </TextBlock>

        <TextBlock Text="Reading the panels" Style="{StaticResource SectionHeader}" FontSize="15" />
        <TextBlock TextWrapping="Wrap" Margin="0,0,0,12">
          The left panel shows file identity — name, path, size, modification time and SHA-256 — above the
          structure tree. Selecting a node fills the <Bold>Details</Bold> grid with that node's fields.
          <LineBreak /><LineBreak />
          <Bold>Summary</Bold> lists the document-level properties and counts.
          <Bold>Raw text</Bold> shows the decoded text payload.
          <Bold>Hex</Bold> is a paged offset / hexadecimal / ASCII dump read directly from disk, so a large
          file does not have to fit in memory.
          <Bold>Messages</Bold> collects every warning raised while reading, including anything that was
          truncated by a safety limit.
        </TextBlock>

        <TextBlock Text="Searching" Style="{StaticResource SectionHeader}" FontSize="15" />
        <TextBlock TextWrapping="Wrap" Margin="0,0,0,12">
          Type in the <Bold>Find</Bold> box and press Enter. Three modes are available:
          <LineBreak />
          • <Bold>Text</Bold> — matches node names, field names and field values.<LineBreak />
          • <Bold>Field name</Bold> — matches field names only.<LineBreak />
          • <Bold>Hex bytes</Bold> — matches a byte pattern against the raw file, written as
          <Bold>50 4B 03 04</Bold>, <Bold>504B0304</Bold> or <Bold>50-4B-03-04</Bold>. Use
          <Bold>??</Bold> for a byte that may be anything.
          <LineBreak /><LineBreak />
          Results appear on the <Bold>Search results</Bold> tab. Selecting a tree hit jumps to that node;
          selecting a hex hit jumps to that page of the dump.
        </TextBlock>

        <TextBlock Text="Exporting and copying" Style="{StaticResource SectionHeader}" FontSize="15" />
        <TextBlock TextWrapping="Wrap" Margin="0,0,0,12">
          <Bold>File ▸ Export</Bold> writes the parsed document as JSON, CSV or plain text. JSON keeps the
          tree shape; CSV flattens it to one row per field; text is an indented outline. Every export
          records the source file's SHA-256 so a result can be tied back to the exact input.
          <LineBreak /><LineBreak />
          <Bold>Edit ▸ Copy selected node</Bold> puts the selected node and its fields on the clipboard.
          Grids also support Ctrl+C on a selection.
        </TextBlock>

        <TextBlock Text="Editing a file" Style="{StaticResource SectionHeader}" FontSize="15" />
        <TextBlock TextWrapping="Wrap" Margin="0,0,0,12">
          Files always open read-only. Tick <Bold>Edit mode</Bold> on the toolbar, or
          <Bold>Edit ▸ Enable editing</Bold>, to allow changes. Nothing on disk changes until you save.
          <LineBreak /><LineBreak />
          With edit mode on, the <Bold>Value</Bold> column in Details and Summary becomes typeable.
          Changed rows are highlighted and marked with a dot, and the <Bold>Original</Bold> column keeps
          showing what the file held. Greyed rows cannot be edited: a declared .NET type name, a value
          that was too long to show in full, or a value the reader derived rather than read.
          <LineBreak /><LineBreak />
          A change is refused outright only when it could not be written to a readable file — for example
          a character XML cannot carry. When a value's <Italic>kind</Italic> changes, such as a True/False
          field being given free text, the change is allowed but a warning appears in
          <Bold>Messages</Bold>: this build has no schema for the format and will not pretend to know
          your configuration is wrong.
        </TextBlock>

        <TextBlock Text="Reviewing and saving changes" Style="{StaticResource SectionHeader}" FontSize="15" />
        <TextBlock TextWrapping="Wrap" Margin="0,0,0,12">
          The <Bold>Pending changes</Bold> tab lists every edit as <Italic>was → becomes</Italic>, with where
          in the document it sits. Revert one from there, or revert everything at once.
          <LineBreak /><LineBreak />
          <Bold>File ▸ Save as RPE…</Bold> (Ctrl+Shift+S) writes a new .rpe ready to import into OPC Router.
          The file you opened is not touched.
          <LineBreak />
          <Bold>File ▸ Save (overwrite original)…</Bold> replaces the open file. It asks first and copies the
          original to a timestamped <Bold>.bak</Bold> beside it before writing.
          <LineBreak /><LineBreak />
          Either way the new file is written to a temporary name, re-opened and re-parsed to check it is
          readable, and only then moved into place. A save that fails that check writes nothing. The
          confirmation shows the size and SHA-256 of what was written.
          <LineBreak /><LineBreak />
          A save with no changes reproduces the original's contents byte for byte, so an exported file
          differs only where you edited it.
          <LineBreak /><LineBreak />
          That is checked for each file as it is opened rather than taken on trust. If a file cannot be
          reproduced exactly — most often because it holds carriage returns inside values, which every
          XML reader is required to convert to line feeds — a warning appears in <Bold>Messages</Bold>
          naming the reason, and the save confirmation repeats it. Editing still works; you simply know
          in advance that the file will differ in that one respect as well as where you edited it.
        </TextBlock>

        <TextBlock Text="When a file is not recognised" Style="{StaticResource SectionHeader}" FontSize="15" />
        <TextBlock TextWrapping="Wrap" Margin="0,0,0,12">
          If no structural parser matches, the generic inspector takes over. It reports only what can be
          measured — file signature, encoding, Shannon entropy, container listing and printable strings —
          and states plainly that the layout is unknown. It never invents a field structure.
          Such a document is always read-only: this build will not write a file whose layout it has not
          established. The same applies when a safety limit stopped the document being read in full.
        </TextBlock>

        <TextBlock Text="Logs" Style="{StaticResource SectionHeader}" FontSize="15" />
        <TextBlock TextWrapping="Wrap">
          Errors are written to a dated file under the <Bold>Logs</Bold> folder beside the executable, or
          under %LOCALAPPDATA%\RPEReader\Logs when that location is not writable. Use
          <Bold>Help ▸ Open log folder</Bold> to find it. Logs contain file names and error text only —
          never data read out of a .rpe file — so they are safe to attach to a support request.
        </TextBlock>

      </StackPanel>
    </ScrollViewer>

    <Button Grid.Row="1" Content="Close" Width="90" Height="28" Margin="0,12,0,0"
            HorizontalAlignment="Right" IsDefault="True" IsCancel="True" Click="OnCloseClick" />
  </Grid>
</Window>
